@@front Use of Generative AI

I used generative AI as a supporting tool alongside my own work. It was never the author of the system or of this report. Its contribution was limited to the following:

- Debugging. When a simulator configuration failed or a policy learned the wrong behaviour, I used an assistant to help read stack traces and narrow down candidate causes. Every fix was verified against the running system before I kept it.
- Exploring implementation options. I asked for comparisons between approaches I was already considering — for example, whether to embed Isaac Sim in the backend process or run it as a subprocess. The decisions, and the reasons recorded in Chapter 5, are mine.
- Boilerplate and documentation. I used it to draft repetitive code and docstrings so that I could spend my time on the morphology, expression and audit layers that carry the project's contribution.
- Language and formatting. I used it to tighten wording and to help prepare this document. The structure, the argument and the claims are mine.

Every suggestion I accepted was checked against the source code, the repository history, the recorded telemetry or the cited literature. Where a generated suggestion disagreed with what the system actually did, I kept the system. Responsibility for the technical content of this report and for the submitted work is mine.

