@@pagebreak

@@front Abstract

Training a robot with reinforcement learning normally means assembling six separate things: a physics simulator, an environment implementation, a reward function, a learning library, experiment monitoring and a way to evaluate the resulting policy. Each one assumes you already understand the other five. I built EasyRTG to collapse that assembly into a single guided workflow. The user loads a robot in Universal Robot Description Format (URDF), describes the behaviour they want in plain language, reviews a configuration that an assistant has drafted, tests the reward before spending GPU time, launches parallel training and replays the recorded policy — without writing simulator or reinforcement-learning code.

The finished system combines a Flutter desktop interface, a FastAPI orchestration backend, PyBullet for robot inspection and reward testing, and NVIDIA Isaac Sim for parallel training and playback. Its central technical decision is to stop hard-coding robots. Instead of joint-name rules and a fixed reward menu, EasyRTG derives control-relevant morphology from the URDF itself and represents every reward term as an audited mathematical expression over a shared state vocabulary. That one change is what allowed the same application pipeline to train quadrupeds and a humanoid without a separate implementation per body.

I evaluated the platform on 40 timestamped Isaac run directories collected between 13 July and 18 August 2026. Thirty-four contained usable reward telemetry; six failed before producing any. The experiments cover the Unitree A1, ANYmal, Unitree Go1 and NASA Valkyrie with PPO, SAC and TD3. Twelve runs held at least 95% of the 1,000-step episode cap. Peak throughput was 32,056.8 environment steps per second on a single RTX 5000. Go1 reached 96.3%, 100% and 99.1% of the cap with PPO, SAC and TD3 respectively. Valkyrie reached 82.1% with SAC but only about 20% with PPO and TD3 — the same robot, the same reward expression, the same step budget, and a different outcome purely because of the learner.

Those numbers cut both ways, and the report says so. GPU parallelism is what made the experiments possible at all. It also made it easy to produce a high reward that did not mean the intended behaviour: on the galloping task, TD3 scored well with a fast trot that never left the ground. What EasyRTG demonstrates is a working integration of language-assisted configuration, general robot inspection and accelerated learning. What it does not yet demonstrate is reduced cognitive load — that needs a user study — or transfer to physical hardware. Those two gaps define the next stage of the work.

