Six timestamped run directories contained no numeric reward telemetry and are retained in the failure count rather than discarded. One further directory, named `p3gallop`, does not follow the timestamped `*-isaac` naming convention and is excluded from all fleet-level calculations. Every figure in this report is generated from the run archive at build time by `scripts/make_report_figures.py`; the run directories themselves remain the authoritative data.

# Appendix C  Supplementary Figures

These figures support Chapter 6 but were moved here to keep the main body within the page limit. Each is generated from the same run archive as the figures in the body.

@@figure fleet_chronology.png 6.2x2.37 :: Best mean episode length for every analysable Isaac run, in chronological order.

The chronological view is the shape of the project rather than a result. Early ANYmal runs cluster low. The A1 and Go1 runs from late July onward cluster near the cap. The two runs at the far right, at 16 environments, are the 18 August additions that establish the low end of the throughput relationship in Section 6.4.

@@figure learning_curves.png 6.2x3.9 :: Reward against environment steps for the best run of each robot family.

Plotted at the same scale within each panel, the four best runs show how differently the four bodies behave. A1 rises steeply and then jumps again near 16M steps. Go1 climbs in visible stages. ANYmal is unstable throughout, dipping by half between 3M and 5M steps before recovering — the clearest single picture of why ANYmal needed ten attempts. Valkyrie improves slowly for 25M steps and only then accelerates, which is why a shorter budget would have concluded that the humanoid could not learn at all.

@@figure sample_efficiency.png 6.2x3.5 :: Environment steps before each run's smoothed mean episode length first holds above 900.

@@figure go1_episode_length.png 6.2x2.37 :: Episode length for the best Go1 run under each learner.

The Go1 episode-length panel is the companion to Section 6.6: all three learners reach and hold the cap, so on this robot survival does not distinguish them at all. Compare it against the Valkyrie equivalent in Section 6.8, where survival is the only thing that does.

