| 20260818-084821 | Go1 | SAC | 16 | 97.5 | 121.1 | 12.1% | 96 |
| 20260818-145041 | Go1 | PPO | 16 | 147.2 | 250.4 | 25.0% | 80 |

The twelve runs that hold at least 95% of the episode cap are the five A1 PPO runs from 27-28 July, the single ANYmal PPO run of 17 July at 2,048 environments, and all six Go1 runs at 4,096 environments or above. No Valkyrie run reaches that threshold under any learner.

# Appendix E  Compute Environment and Reproduction Notes

Every training and evaluation result in this report was produced on one workstation, and the pre-release status of the simulator stack is relevant to reproducing them.

Table: Compute and software environment used for all experiments.
| Component | Version or specification |
|---|---|
| GPU | One NVIDIA RTX 5000 |
| Isaac Sim | 6.0.1-rc.7 |
| Isaac Lab | 3.0.0-beta2 (`v3.0.0-beta2.patch1`) |
| Physics backend | Newton (replaces `omni.physics.tensors` from Isaac Sim 6.0) |
| Isaac Sim Python | 3.12, bundled with Isaac Sim |
| Application Python | 3.12, project virtual environment |
| Inspection simulator | PyBullet, headless |
| Backend | FastAPI |
| Desktop client | Flutter |
| Episode cap | 1,000 steps |

Both Isaac components are pre-release: Isaac Sim is a release candidate and Isaac Lab is a beta. This matters for reproduction, because the physics backend changed to Newton in Isaac Sim 6.0 and results obtained on an earlier `omni.physics.tensors` build should not be expected to match. Isaac Sim also owns its own bundled Python and therefore cannot run inside the application's virtual environment, which is the practical reason for the subprocess architecture described in Section 4.5 rather than a stylistic preference.

Three further notes for anyone reproducing this work. First, throughput figures are peak values observed within a run, not sustained averages, and they depend on environment count as shown in Section 6.4. Second, the two runs at 16 environments were deliberately small diagnostic runs and should not be read as failed training attempts. Third, all figures in this report are regenerated from `backend/runs` by `scripts/make_report_figures.py`, so adding runs to the archive and re-running the figure and document scripts updates the evidence without any manual editing.
